Note = {CIANS-ISBM Satellite Conference, HANNOVER, GERMANY, JUL 12-14, 1992},
Organization = {COLLEGIUM INT ACTIVITATIS NERVOSAE SUPER; INT SOC BIOPHYS MED},
Abstract = {Over the past seven years we have studied the effects of stress
   management interventions in Human Immunodeficiency Virus-Type 1 (HIV-1)
   infection. Our findings suggest that behavioral interventions such as
   Cognitive Behavioral Stress Management (CBSM) may reduce distress and
   depression and normalize immunological status for HIV+ men during the
   stressful period immediately following notification of HIV+ status and
   during the adjustment period following this news. Results suggest that
   these effects may be mediated by increases in social support perceptions
   and utilization, increased use of active coping strategies (e.g.
   relaxation exercises, active coping, and planning), and decreased use of
   denial/avoidance coping strategies. What is critical to learn at this
   point is first, whether this time-limited intervention and the
   implementation of a regular group maintenance program can help gay men
   adjust to the chronic burden of symptomatic HIV infection. Second, it
   will be essential to determine if intervention-associated psychosocial
   and immunologic changes predict a delay in the progression of clinical
   disease. We present an empirically-derived model proposing how CBSM
   might affect the progression of HTV-1 infection.},
