   ITALY, JUL 10-15, 2001},
Organization = {Int Assoc Geochem \& Cosmochem; Italian Natl Res Council; Univ Cagliari;
   Soc Geochim Italiana},
Abstract = {Acid mine drainage discharged from the abandoned tailings in the Daduk
   mine has a pH of 3.3, and concentrations of Al, Mri, Fe, Zn and SO4 of
   18, 41, 45, 38 and 1940 mg/L, respectively. In particular, As
   concentration in acid mine drainage is 1,000 mug/L. Removing order of
   metal ions normalized by SO4 concentration downstream from discharge
   point is Fe > As > Al > Cu > Zn > Mn > Cd > Pb. This indicates that in
   the Daduk creek, concentrations of Fe, As and Al are decreased by the
   formation of precipitates as well as dilution, but concentrations of
   heavy metals except these ions by mainly dilution. From the results of
   geochemical modeling using the Phreeq C program, goethite (FeOOH) is
   oversaturated, and schwertmannite (Fe8O8(OH)(4.5)(SO4)(1.75)) is the
   most stable solid phase at low pH in the Daduk creek. Thus, yellowish
   red (orange ochre) precipitates occurred in the study area seem either
   to be goethite or schwertmannite. In case of water samples with white
   precipitates, the positive saturation indices of gibbsite (Al(OH)(3))
   and basaluminite (Al-4(SO4)(OH)(10). 5H(2)O) indicate possible
   precipitation.},
