Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {The teaching of Occupational Health and Safety (OHS) requires modern
   teaching methodologies to assist in analyzing and understanding issues
   within the professional scope of future practitioners. Throughout the
   application of these strategies, it is necessary to assess how measure
   the skills acquired by the student described in the literature. This
   study investigates the forms of knowledge assessment that should be
   applied when active learning strategies are used in this field. A
   narrative literature review identified five primary forms of student
   knowledge assessment: learning styles, students' perception of learning,
   pre-and post-tests, existing tools and methodologies, and the creation
   of assessment instruments. The assessment methods vary from
   questionnaires for quantitative analysis to interviews for qualitative
   analysis. Additionally, approaches to evaluating students' learning
   styles were observed, such as Kolb's Experiment, the Index of Learning
   Styles (ILS), and Myers-Briggs indicators. Although gaps were identified
   in recommending specific forms of competence assessment in OHS within
   engineering, OHS curriculum components in various engineering
   disciplines underscore the importance of future research in this
   combined area with education.},
