Note = {International Conference on Global Public Health, Int Ctr Res \& Dev Sri
   Lanka, Colombo, SRI LANKA, DEC 03-04, 2012},
Organization = {Umea Univ},
Abstract = {The current paper explores the prevalence and popularity of folk healing
   in and around the village of Sawrakoti, situated in Himachal Pradesh,
   India. It looks into the continuing presence of and dependence on folk
   healing and its specialists, in spite of the presence of
   institutionalized health care such as a primary health centre (PHC) and
   government hospitals providing subsidized treatment. Sawrakoti has come
   up as a major educational hub in the region, attracting students and
   workers from villages near and far. This influx of people has
   transformed the area, with increased access to facilities like
   electricity and telecommunications and a greater geographical
   connectivity. The paper investigates factors which influence and inform
   the interaction of the local people with folk healers, such as the
   notion of familiarity, inefficacy of available medical treatments, local
   perceptions of divine and supernatural involvement in health and
   well-being, problems with family and other social relations and attempts
   to ensure success of on-going treatments. It endeavours to find the
   various causes for folk healing being a prominent feature of the
   region's cultural landscape.},
