   12-21, 2000},
Organization = {NATO},
Abstract = {The thalamus is the main gate for sensory information on its way to the
   cortex. Since the most of sensory information processing and conscious
   perception of external world is done at the cortical level the thalamus
   represents a key link in this process. The dorsal lateral geniculate
   nucleus is the best-explored thalamic structure. The neuronal elements
   of the thalamus can be divided into three components: the extrinsic
   afferent inputs to the nucleus, the relay cells (principal neurons)
   projecting to the cortex, and the interneurons. Extrinsic afferents can
   be divided into two classes: driving inputs, representing primary
   information to be relayed to the cortex, and modulatory inputs-all other
   inputs, modulating and/or controlling the relay of information from
   driving inputs to the cortex. Several groups of modulatory afferents can
   be distinguished depending on the neurotransmitters used: glutamatergic
   (cortical), cholinergic/nitrergic (from brainstem parabrachial region,
   nucleus basalis and parabigeminal nucleus), GABAcrgic (from thalamic
   reticular nucleus/perigeniculate nucleus and pretectal nuclei),
   serotonergic (from dorsal raphe), noradrenergic (from locus coeruleus)
   and histaminergic (from posterior hypothalamus).},
