   Wolverhampton, Wolverhampton, ENGLAND, MAY 09-12, 1999},
Organization = {Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Appl Sci; Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Engn; Univ
   Wolverhampton, Built Environm},
Abstract = {Recent advances in computing power have enabled rapid processing of
   large data sets, leading to the burgeoning use of computer simulation in
   many fields. Perhaps due to the specialised nature of both software
   engineering and experimental weathering science, and to traditional
   differences in the cultures of both fields, researchers in the field of
   rock weathering have yet to take full advantage of such new technology.
   This chapter discusses the development and use of computer simulation
   programs in the study of rock weathering. Illustration is provided via
   ``DK{''}, a simple weathering simulation program, currently in
   development. The main advantages of computer simulation experiments are
   the transparency of the processes leading to the results and the
   logistical ease with which experiments can be performed, including many
   experiments that would be impossible to carry out in the real world. The
   main disadvantages are those common to many other research techniques,
   including the time taken to learn, the need for careful validation and
   verification and the initial expense of equipment. The tasks involved in
   developing a simulation model are discussed, as are some possibilities
   for future developments in weathering simulations, including the
   accurate prediction of masonry lifetimes in a variety of environments.},
